<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Teutonic War (1326–1332)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1326">1326</a>–<a href="/years/1332">1332</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Teutonic War of 1326–1332 was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> under Władysław I the Elbow-high and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, which the knights had seized in 1308. The Order, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John of Bohemia</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> fought it to a draw at Płowce in 1331. It ended with a truce in 1332 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kalisz-1343">Treaty of Kalisz</a> in 1343.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-teutonic-war-1326-1332.jpg" alt="Polish–Teutonic War (1326–1332)" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Karl von Spruner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Europe_in_the_early_14th_century..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> on the Baltic coast was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samborides">Samborides</a>, liensmen of the Polish Piast rulers, until Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a> died in 1294. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1295, took it into the Polish Crown against the protest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and was killed the next year in a kidnapping instigated by the Ascanian margraves. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>, which had ruled Poland under Wenceslaus II, died out in 1306, Władysław I the Elbow-high occupied Pomerelia, but the Swenzones family called in Margrave Waldemar of Brandenburg.</p><p>Władysław then summoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, whose forces under Heinrich von Plötzke retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> in 1308. When he refused to pay their costs, they kept the land and in 1309 bought Waldemar's claims by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-soldin">Treaty of Soldin</a>. Władysław sued the Order at the Roman Curia without success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Crowned in 1320, Władysław allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> by marrying his daughter Elisabeth to King Charles I and his son Casimir to Aldona, daughter of Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>. The Order supported King John the Blind of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, who claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, and was allied with Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-of-plock">Wenceslaus of Płock</a>. In 1326 Polish forces with Lithuanian support devastated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a>, and the next year they turned against the Order while John marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. The knights conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a>; Władysław, with help from Lithuania and from Hungary, whose forces William Drugeth commanded, pillaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Kulmerland</a> up to the Osa River.</p><p>A Polish and Lithuanian counterattack in 1330 brought a temporary peace in which the Order returned part of its gains, but the fighting went on. After the indecisive Battle of Płowce in 1331 the knights retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and Dobrzyń, and both sides agreed an armistice. Władysław died in 1333.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> of 1343, signed by Władysław's son Casimir III, settled the claims: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and Dobrzyń but lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, though Casimir kept the title Lord and Heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Pomerelia and Polish access to the Baltic remained in dispute for centuries, straining German–Polish relations: the region was regained as Royal Prussia after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466, annexed by the Kingdom of Prussia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772, and made part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-corridor">Polish Corridor</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> in 1919.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Teutonic War (1326–1332)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
